  stickyNoteInfos:
  - title: 'Sample Mesh Operator:'
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: 78
      y: 597
      width: 493
      height: 837
    contents: "The sample mesh operator allows you to get the position of triangles,
      vertices, and edges of the sampled mesh.\r\n\r\nFirst, we reference the mesh
      we want to sample.\r\nThen we set the placement mode to \u201Csurface,\u201D
      as we want to access the mesh triangles.\r\n\r\nAs we to spawn from all the
      triangles, we're creating a random number between index 0 and the mesh triangle
      count.\r\n\r\nWe're using two different other random numbers so that each particle
      is spawned randomly on the surface of each triangle.\r\n\r\nThe position can
      be used directly to set the particle's position.\r\n\r\nWe're also getting
      the color.x component, which contains ambient occlusion values that have been
      baked into the mesh.\r\n"
    theme: Black
    textSize: Medium
  - title: 'Distance to Plane:'
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: 1689
      y: 607
      width: 336
      height: 100
    contents: We're using a distance-to-plane operator. This operator computes the
      distance value from the plane to each particle's position, creating a gradient
      that will be used to dissolve our particles
    theme: Black
    textSize: Small
  - title: 'Lifetime:'
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: -2014
      y: 1991
      width: 297
      height: 121
    contents: 'As we want our mesh to gradually dissolve, we''re adjusting the lifetime
      attribute based on the distance values previously computed.

      Therefore,
      particles closer to the plane will die before those that are further away.

'
    theme: Black
    textSize: Small
  - title: 'Dissolve Animation:'
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: -2907
      y: 3047
      width: 259
      height: 121
    contents: With the help of a smoothstep operator, we're creating an animated
      mask based on the lifetime of our particles. This mask is used to drive the
      wind strength, turbulence amplitude, and color of our particles.
    theme: Black
    textSize: Small
  - title: 'Wind Force:'
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: -1590
      y: 2701
      width: 247
      height: 100
    contents: A strong wind force affects the particles when the dissolve mask is
      on.
    theme: Black
    textSize: Small
  - title: Dissolve Sampled Mesh
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: 2281
      y: -402
      width: 932
      height: 100
    contents: type something here
    theme: Black
    textSize: Huge
  - title: Color
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: -2006
      y: 3441
      width: 257
      height: 130
    contents: "The ambient occlusion of the mesh has been baked into its vertex colors.
      \r\nThis data has been sampled and stored in a custom particle attribute named
      \"AO.\u201D\r\nWe use this data to sample a color gradient and set our particle's
      colors.\r\n"
    theme: Black
    textSize: Small
  - title: Rotate Center Distance
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: -1927
      y: 4527
      width: 326
      height: 146
    contents: "We first calculate the distance from a rotation's center point. This
      float value will be used to drive the rotation of our particles\u2019 position
      around this rotation\u2019s center. The closer the particles are to the center,
      the faster they will rotate."
    theme: Black
    textSize: Small
  - title: Rotation Amount
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: -501
      y: 5056
      width: 292
      height: 140
    contents: "The rotation amount is also modulated by age over lifetime. This allows
      the rotation to be controlled during the particle\u2019s lifetime. This modulation
      is used by multiplying the previously calculated distance to the rotation center.
      In the end, the rotation amount is modulated by the distance to the rotation
      center and also by its age over its lifetime."
    theme: Black
    textSize: Small
  - title: Rotate 3D
    position:
      serializedVersion: 2
      x: -219
      y: 4745
      width: 290
      height: 129
    contents: The Rotate 3D operator allows us to rotate the particle's position
      around a position. Doing this in the update is practical as we're not interfering
      with the forces in the update context, but are adding some post-transformation
      to our particles positions.
    theme: Black
    textSize: Small
  categories: []
  uiBounds:
    serializedVersion: 2
    x: -2907
    y: -402
    width: 8923
    height: 5644
